hermes v0.23.0: memory.background_consolidation = "auto" lets the
unattended background review consolidate memory without /memory approve.

Upstream (tools/memory_tool.py, _background_delete_gate, #105921): the
background-review fork may only `add`; every `patch`/`remove` it issues
(single or in a batch) is staged in $HERMES_HOME/pending/memory for a
human. Once MEMORY.md (2200 chars) or USER.md (1375) is full, the
review's consolidation therefore waits until someone runs /memory
pending + approve, and new facts stop landing. The foreground gate
(memory.write_approval) is a separate switch and already off.

New key memory.background_consolidation, default "stage" (upstream's
behaviour, unchanged). With "auto", and only while memory.write_approval
is off, the gate instead:
  - pins each op to the full entry its old_text selects now
    (_pin_matched_entries, as staging does); no match = the same error
    the direct write returns, nothing written;
  - removal cap: a consolidation that would remove more than 50% of the
    store's entries, or shrink the file by more than 40% of its
    characters, is staged as under "stage" (logged with the reason);
  - snapshots the target file to memories/.history/<utc>-MEMORY.md
    (or -USER.md), 0600 in a 0700 directory; no new snapshot when the
    newest one is identical; kept: every snapshot of the last 30 days
    plus the newest 50 per file. A failed snapshot falls back to staging;
  - applies through apply_memory_pending (the /memory approve replay),
    so only the pinned entries change;
  - logs one line to the agent log and a JSON line to
    memories/.history/consolidations.jsonl (0600).
Foreground turns and attended (/refine) reviews are untouched.

Restore: a consolidations.jsonl line's "backup" is the file as it was
before that consolidation. As root, with $H the agent's HERMES_HOME:
  install -m600 -o <agent> -g users $H/memories/.history/<ts>-MEMORY.md \
    $H/memories/MEMORY.md.new && mv $H/memories/MEMORY.md.new $H/memories/MEMORY.md
(USER.md alike). Running sessions keep the memory in their prompt until
their next session.

replay_background_pending() settles records staged before "auto" was on:
oldest first, background_review-origin only, each replayed through
apply_memory_pending after a snapshot. Applied records are removed. A
record that no longer matches (stale/unpinned) is first written whole,
payload and its adds included, to memories/.history/dropped/<id>.json
(0600), logged, then removed; if that copy fails it stays pending.
Kept pending for the next start: foreground records, records over the
removal cap, and any whose snapshot, store read (an I/O error, not a
mismatch) or write failed. A no-op unless the mode is "auto".
hyperhermes calls it from the agent unit's preStart
(../replay-memory-pending.py, under one 30 s timeout for all homes).

Tests: tests/tools/test_memory_background_consolidation.py.

